#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175621 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "06828f66-a8be-59f5-b82d-afd93333f38d" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11006" :question/problem "Which of the following statements regarding the graph of $y=\\sec x$ are true?\n\n1. The function takes the value $1$ at $x = 2n\\pi$, where $n$ is an integer\n2. The range of the function is $(-\\infty,+\\infty)$\n3. The function has zeros at $x = 0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2 \\pi,\\dots$" :question/worked-solution "Let's have a look at the graph of $y=\\sec x$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3563/Source/Images/6d04c6687b88309d564d8222e10787db.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- The secant takes the value $1$ at $x = 2 n\\pi$. Therefore, statement I is true.\n- The range of $f(x) = \\sec x$ is $[MATH: (-\\infty,-1] \\cup [1, \\infty)]$. Therefore, statement II is false.\n- The secant graph does not intersect the $x$-axis. Therefore, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175622 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce45704e-ecea-5c67-bb40-a413f8a0d72e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f76b3e8f-5580-5cb0-8d6c-85711c446602" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11007" :question/problem "If $\\sec x =2 \\sqrt 2$ then what is $\\sec(x+8\\pi)$?" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y = \\sec x$ is $2\\pi$. Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} = \\sec{(x+2n\\pi)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sec x &= \\sec (x + 2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot π) \\\\\n&= \\sec (x + 8π)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} = 2\\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\sec{(x+8\\pi)} = 2\\sqrt 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175629 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4b378b0-30e5-59a8-b9f7-882b5e226a2e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Secant Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6bd67fa4-16b5-5505-bfc5-35a00f54e980" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11014" :question/problem "If $\\sec{x} = -\\dfrac{11}{6}$, then $\\sec(-x - 14\\pi) =$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\sec{x}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3563/Source/Images/54de4a90efd6ebeccfdace7b62890f94.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\sec{x}$ is $2\\pi$.\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\sec{x}$ is an *even* function, and we have\n$\\sec(-x) = \\sec{x}$.\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\sec{x}$, we have\n$\\sec (-x - 14π)|= \\sec (-x - 2π \\cdot 7); = \\sec (-x)$.\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\sec{x}$, we have\n$\\sec (-x)|= \\sec x; =-\\frac{11}{6}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sec(-x - 14\\pi) = -\\dfrac{11}{6}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246907 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f8f5672-e1aa-50d2-9c3d-91d2ee1de8fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246909} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9a74b3-f6d5-5fe1-aeb6-77136a47979b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246910} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc0de06-c2b0-5afe-bdd2-8f609cfcc627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246911} {:answer/id #uuid "46f75a7c-a905-572f-a688-1ce7ccbcbdb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246912} {:answer/id #uuid "768b4b31-9fff-5867-9a51-a1d11b233235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46f75a7c-a905-572f-a688-1ce7ccbcbdb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246912} :answer-field/id #uuid "495ebc73-ac79-584f-9c99-4ee9a9ad9074" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246908}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39a74574-7744-5736-a3fd-45ef093bd5c0" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103685" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\n- The range of the function is $y=(-\\infty ,-1]$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is an output of the function\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is not a zero of the function" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934039/assets/d0f3e760be991c936baf5e9825cd582f086a73a8330032d24fd35fd5594b4864.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).$ Therefore, $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not an output of the function.\n\n- Statement III is true since the function has no zeros."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce45704e-ecea-5c67-bb40-a413f8a0d72e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "257d8569-1596-5447-96fe-73f1729cb4f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246916} {:answer/id #uuid "cc058cc2-6299-55de-91fa-ec39d31c2150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246917} {:answer/id #uuid "d60635c1-223f-53ea-b9a0-dae4c08def05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246918} {:answer/id #uuid "73e9d5d7-f804-515b-867e-7743155ba41b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246919} {:answer/id #uuid "522b9b53-d6de-5474-91be-2c85f047a459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d60635c1-223f-53ea-b9a0-dae4c08def05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246918} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a71cce0-0d24-5b37-b589-91ec75d64121" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246915}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2f84c41-54d9-5d26-a43f-85f7af5ae1ae" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103691" :question/problem "If $sec⁡x=3\\sqrt{3},$ then what is $sec⁡(x+8\\pi )?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=4$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2\\cdot 4\\cdot \\pi )=sec⁡(x+8\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec⁡x=3\\sqrt{3},$ then $sec⁡(x+8\\pi )=3\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce45704e-ecea-5c67-bb40-a413f8a0d72e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0859139a-ae82-5848-a3b4-ee23e730eec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246923} {:answer/id #uuid "b139bbac-3b13-5258-835a-d13db759f2e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246924} {:answer/id #uuid "49f5fa12-9bbb-5820-9be1-e87d3806aa84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246925} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7ec9b0-8a3b-5cc9-9199-27dd41d9d39d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246926} {:answer/id #uuid "0f754937-914f-59f7-bbed-df3699d7d31d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd7ec9b0-8a3b-5cc9-9199-27dd41d9d39d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246926} :answer-field/id #uuid "6456bd4d-7629-5136-a81a-9af7cf729aa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246922}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6d7d5eb-1b94-5b00-99dd-ee49c1d6dfaf" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103692" :question/problem "If $sec⁡x=\\sqrt{7},$ then what is $sec⁡(x-16\\pi )?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-8$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2\\cdot (-8)\\cdot \\pi )=sec⁡(x-16\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec⁡x=\\sqrt{7},$ then $sec⁡(x-16\\pi )=\\sqrt{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce45704e-ecea-5c67-bb40-a413f8a0d72e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dfd00e9-38f2-50ae-ba29-d9e6169ae111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246930} {:answer/id #uuid "95c5c9b0-0419-5aa4-80ca-2c01f8aa2bdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246931} {:answer/id #uuid "03d0f9da-3ee2-5c30-92fa-14505ca4ad02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246932} {:answer/id #uuid "49395bd1-9c98-51b3-b77c-6d10e1c6742a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246933} {:answer/id #uuid "e48b367e-ea3b-534f-8139-ccbd17ec8627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03d0f9da-3ee2-5c30-92fa-14505ca4ad02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246932} :answer-field/id #uuid "e537e696-db4f-5020-ad8d-75cd7f1791db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246929}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c667c40e-2c37-506e-8ed3-761b7f4f6b6d" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103701" :question/problem "If $sec⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2},$ then what is $sec⁡(x+14\\pi )?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=7$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2\\cdot 7\\cdot \\pi )=sec⁡(x+14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2},$ then $sec⁡(x+14\\pi )=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce45704e-ecea-5c67-bb40-a413f8a0d72e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "672b8aab-6b54-5332-94d9-88022cf192e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246937} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d2bbb4-01c2-5fbc-b253-6b7233f4add2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246938} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec72097-381b-5e0e-9977-3b0c849d756a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246939} {:answer/id #uuid "99a5203b-a8a8-5dfa-9780-e39174d23215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246940} {:answer/id #uuid "aa86e5a1-565e-502e-bfaa-c81ddba1596a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99a5203b-a8a8-5dfa-9780-e39174d23215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246940} :answer-field/id #uuid "b298d044-0e1c-5362-bce8-97960a068140" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246936}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93b766fa-0a8a-5e1b-959f-e7c8af4049dc" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-103706" :question/problem "If $sec⁡x=2\\sqrt{5},$ then what is $\\sqrt{5}sec⁡(x+20\\pi )-3?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=10$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2\\cdot 10\\cdot \\pi )=sec⁡(x+20\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec⁡x=2\\sqrt{5},$ then $sec⁡(x+20\\pi )=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}sec⁡(x+20\\pi )-3 & =\\sqrt{5}(2\\sqrt{5})-3 \\\\  & =2(5)-3 \\\\  & =10-3 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4b378b0-30e5-59a8-b9f7-882b5e226a2e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "750dfcdd-2a17-51e2-9e7f-6fb3bf9ce64d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246944} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e02c92-e3c0-5414-a2bf-ea36ca5900d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246945} {:answer/id #uuid "657f3603-5fea-59bc-8743-f4a678b78d00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246946} {:answer/id #uuid "a123e890-17d8-59e5-96b1-fc04d8f43471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246947} {:answer/id #uuid "397a5f44-99dc-59c0-af23-7707e8949dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3e02c92-e3c0-5414-a2bf-ea36ca5900d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246945} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9882397-8495-5e27-a125-069a29b3baf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246943}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae3cb1bd-2bc1-5766-9ec9-742fa9428ab0" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-136623" :question/problem "If $sec⁡x=-2,$ then $5sec⁡(-x+12\\pi )+1=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934039/assets/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $sec⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡(-x)=sec⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $sec⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x+12\\pi ) & =sec⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 6) \\\\  & =sec⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $sec⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x) & =sec⁡x \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5sec⁡(-x+12\\pi )+1 & =5\\cdot (-2)+1 \\\\  & =-10+1 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4b378b0-30e5-59a8-b9f7-882b5e226a2e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78971341-d3d4-5d96-b015-52fb0daad5ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246951} {:answer/id #uuid "9b70da4e-5f59-5f9f-b941-70a3e42a6a85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246952} {:answer/id #uuid "975b7c9a-b929-5932-9429-30453b6845ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10+4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246953} {:answer/id #uuid "c4407f67-d36c-5988-a339-34db41d8db8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246954} {:answer/id #uuid "508219d1-a70e-567f-8b95-1e5129a53983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10+4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4407f67-d36c-5988-a339-34db41d8db8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246954} :answer-field/id #uuid "87c8ad36-9b51-573b-94be-52d0eb067867" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246950}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ab9139f-1e03-5afc-8ab4-1c973a648e4c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-136625" :question/problem "If $sec⁡x=-10,$ then $sec⁡(-x+4\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934039/assets/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $sec⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡(-x)=sec⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $sec⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x+4\\pi ) & =sec⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 2) \\\\  & =sec⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $sec⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡(-x) & =sec⁡x \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sec⁡(-x+4\\pi )=-10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4b378b0-30e5-59a8-b9f7-882b5e226a2e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4777d661-2b29-5959-b28e-e62b9b3e7a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246958} {:answer/id #uuid "c295aa84-3d3a-5fbf-b824-401427a4b42e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246959} {:answer/id #uuid "bf1a9cdc-aa37-5d38-a02c-1b5c25b0187d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246960} {:answer/id #uuid "32c81358-b525-5b89-965f-065e8f4d31cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246961} {:answer/id #uuid "738eebe6-3ede-553e-9a53-22290de9c778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "738eebe6-3ede-553e-9a53-22290de9c778"