88b5a992-c86d-51b7-b4a5-20a2ac353a25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246981} {:answer/id #uuid "216fa1e6-7334-5c50-9e49-2756bbbf0983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246982} {:answer/id #uuid "1ebb627c-539f-5f66-b456-fe0ef484b34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2928594-51b1-51c2-898c-8150836c7272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246980} :answer-field/id #uuid "d97a345f-49d1-5374-a7c9-d75a9a0e4a18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246978}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a47b96d-beb0-547b-ac4b-050bce56591a" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-42853" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\n- $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is in the domain.\n- $x=\\pi $ is a root. $$\n- $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ is not in the range of the function." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934039/assets/da729794a0331383debd75be0a4ce4a9f2d4da3286f50bd4f284868996077412.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is defined for all real numbers except the values $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $ for any integer $n,$ thus it is not defined for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has no zeros.\n\n- Statement III is true. The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ so $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ does not belong to the range."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcce9551-1bf3-53ab-8b01-17c725637619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246986} {:answer/id #uuid "e41f976c-c0cc-59e5-951d-fff95897ed6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246987} {:answer/id #uuid "69c34c48-163f-55af-a70c-9fdbc06247aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246988} {:answer/id #uuid "d7802cb8-4a20-52c0-8156-5490f724fd16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246989} {:answer/id #uuid "29b4e736-1ccc-5b2b-9d84-cb4df6e90942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcce9551-1bf3-53ab-8b01-17c725637619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246986} :answer-field/id #uuid "be3c81d5-9851-5889-b60c-5bf6c671960f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246985}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5dbc695-efe0-5d56-91e2-3b17d730ed49" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-42886" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\n- The point $(0,2)$ is in the graph\n- $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote\n- $y=-2$ belongs to the range of the function." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934039/assets/da729794a0331383debd75be0a4ce4a9f2d4da3286f50bd4f284868996077412.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The $y$-intercept of $sec⁡x$ is $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $y=sec⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ thus $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote.\n\n- Statement III is true. The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ so $y=-2$ belongs to the range."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7894e745-56bc-5c45-bb52-b8b9c70f82d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246993} {:answer/id #uuid "08b67a7c-d5f6-52e3-98ad-ecaf516f8dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246994} {:answer/id #uuid "4839f672-026c-5ec6-a1a5-a907fca69106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246995} {:answer/id #uuid "12927978-9de8-52f7-9e4f-e98160711f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246996} {:answer/id #uuid "54ca62da-b759-5f78-b99a-4ffa360539b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4839f672-026c-5ec6-a1a5-a907fca69106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246995} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b7b2caf-f730-5f8a-974a-9676d6be0bbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246992}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7005a5a-08c4-52c9-9ce8-5f273bd76c57" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-47455" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\n- The period of the function is $\\pi .$\n- The function is increasing in the interval $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n- The function has no zeros." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934039/assets/779d7a31646a229492a8165c8212a5c83ad7d37d843c1c1b3b36766a74bc5124.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The period of the function is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function $y=sec⁡x$ is decreasing on $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has no zeros."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12e6da9f-695a-5581-ac67-281ace2ef108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247000} {:answer/id #uuid "5d29c688-3596-54f8-9526-d78739e28b00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247001} {:answer/id #uuid "bfdceccd-ab1c-51df-8e1a-7b54ad56f145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247002} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5e06fd-8337-58ea-a2f2-248a46a33067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247003} {:answer/id #uuid "2e023378-0111-5d57-b1ca-5fcf705a2ad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e5e06fd-8337-58ea-a2f2-248a46a33067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247003} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff1fe016-e077-5886-a3da-8039c1b98073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246999}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d652f1a-c6ee-5796-966d-faf889bb3033" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-65332" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\n- $x=-\\pi $ is not in the domain of the function. $$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is in the range of the function. $$\n- $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934039/assets/da729794a0331383debd75be0a4ce4a9f2d4da3286f50bd4f284868996077412.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is defined for all real numbers except the values $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $ for any integer $n,$ thus it is defined for $x=-\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ so $y=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is not in the range.\n\n- Statement III is true. The function $y=sec⁡x$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $, thus $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce45704e-ecea-5c67-bb40-a413f8a0d72e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2e319ff-1dd3-5bb0-b05e-73e84acea4d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247007} {:answer/id #uuid "877c11cb-6c36-5e95-a988-a3719269b3a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247008} {:answer/id #uuid "0b80670f-8770-511c-84fb-36014be689b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247009} {:answer/id #uuid "020a7fbe-db44-55b7-bd1d-768a0b7028be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247010} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ccddf1-e435-5117-9f3c-1b90ed6b9caf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "877c11cb-6c36-5e95-a988-a3719269b3a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247008} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1e11de8-930a-56cb-ae6f-ffeb1b84a08e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247006}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95e491e4-b856-5f9d-a26b-b1ed227422f5" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-66603" :question/problem "If $sec⁡x=\\frac{5}{2},$ then what is $4sec⁡(x+10\\pi )-3?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=5$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2\\cdot 5\\cdot \\pi )=sec⁡(x+10\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec⁡x=\\frac{5}{2},$ then $sec⁡(x+10\\pi )=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4sec⁡(x+10\\pi )-3 & =4(\\frac{5}{2})-3 \\\\  & =2(5)-3 \\\\  & =10-3 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
